<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Egidian Constitutions</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1357">1357</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Constitutiones Aegidianae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Egidian Constitutions were a legal code for the Papal States issued in 1357 by Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz">Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz</a>, papal legate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. Named after his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegidius">Aegidius</a>, they organised the provinces of the Church's state, the powers of its governors and the rights of its towns. They remained the basic law of the Papal States until 1816.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/egidian-constitutions.jpg" alt="Egidian Constitutions" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fondazione Mansutti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aegidianae_Constitutiones,_1571_-_002.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
